{"name":"White Bass","hex":"#e8efec","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white bass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Morone chrysops, a freshwater fish of the family Moronidae, found in the eastern and central United States and adjoining Canada."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_bass"}],"entries":[{"title":"White bass","blocks":[{"text":"The white bass, silver bass, or sand bass is a freshwater fish of the temperate bass family Moronidae, and is commonly around 12–15 inches long. The species' main color is silver-white to pale green. Its back is dark, with white sides and belly, and with narrow dark stripes running lengthwise on its sides. It has large, rough scales and two dorsal fins. They are widely distributed across North America, inhabiting large reservoirs and rivers. When mating in the spring, they are more often found in shallow rivers, creeks, and streams. They have been introduced in some places as sport fish and also to predate on nuisance fish, such as gizzard shad. It is the state fish of Oklahoma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/White_Bass_%28Morone_chrysops%29.jpg/330px-White_Bass_%28Morone_chrysops%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_bass"},{"title":"Bass (fish)","blocks":[{"text":"Bass is a common name shared by many species of ray-finned fish from the large clade Percomorpha, mainly belonging to the orders Perciformes and Moroniformes, encompassing both freshwater and marine species. The word bass comes from Middle English bars, meaning \"perch\", despite that none of the commonly referred bass species belong to the perch family Percidae."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bass_(fish)"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/White_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG/500px-White_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/White_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG/1280px-White_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/White_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG/500px-White_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A white bass, caught in Grosse Pointe Woods, MI.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_Bass%2C_Caught_and_Released.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/StripedBass.JPG/500px-StripedBass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/StripedBass.JPG/1280px-StripedBass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/StripedBass.JPG/500px-StripedBass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Striped bass (Morone saxatilis)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStripedBass.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Sea_Bass_%28s